test.annotated_submission:
Since nobody ever dies, and since everyone has at most two children, and since the gender ratio is 1:1, every generation will have exactly 1000 births (since each child has two parents). \textcolor{green}{Good observation that the number of births per generation equals the number of parents, but see next comment for a key issue.}

Therefore, initial growth is 1000 births every 30 years, which is a growth rate of $1000 / 30 \approx 33.33$ births per year. \textcolor{red}{This assumes population grows by 1000 every 30 years, but since nobody dies and each person can have at most two children, the population cannot grow indefinitely at a constant rate. The maximum number of children per person is 2, so the population can at most double each generation, not increase by a fixed 1000. The assumption of constant 1000 births per generation is incorrect.}

After a billion years, the population will be roughly $1000 \times 10^9 / 30 \approx 3.33 \times 10^{10}$ inhabitants. \textcolor{red}{This linear extrapolation is invalid because population growth is not linear here; it should be exponential (doubling each generation) until the cap is reached.}

This is clearly more than $10^{10}$, so a Malthusian catastrophe will occur. The first such catastrophe will occur after $10^{10} / 33.33 \approx 300$ million years. \textcolor{red}{Since the growth rate calculation is incorrect, this timing estimate is also incorrect. The population will reach $10^{10}$ much faster due to exponential growth.}

It will reduce the population to about $10^9$ people, the vast majority of whom will already have had two children; assuming deaths in the catastrophic state are random, the first Malthusian catastrophe reduces future births to $\approx 3.33$ per year, because it reduces the remaining legally breeding population by a factor of 10. \textcolor{yellow}{Good to consider catastrophe effects, but the assumption that the breeding population reduces by a factor of 10 and thus births reduce proportionally needs justification. Also, if everyone already had two children, no further births would occur unless new individuals are born after the catastrophe.}

A 700-million year climb follows which takes the final population to $\approx 10^9 + 3.33 \cdot 7 \cdot 10^8 \approx 2.33 \cdot 10^9$. \textcolor{red}{This linear growth assumption after catastrophe is inconsistent with the model of two children per person and no deaths. Population growth should be modeled as doubling per generation until the cap is reached again.}

In the long term, catastrophe states repeat at ever-increasing intervals, each reduces the breeding population by 90 percent, and the population finally hits a steady state at roughly one billion inhabitants. \textcolor{green}{Good qualitative insight that catastrophes regulate population, but the steady state population estimate is not well supported by the calculations above. A more precise model would consider exponential growth capped by the catastrophe threshold and the effect of the two-child limit.}

\textcolor{green}{Overall, the key mistake is treating population growth as linear rather than exponential under the two-child limit and no deaths. The student should reconsider the growth model and recalculate the timing and population sizes accordingly.}
